course: euphemisms
lesson: 3
title: Soft words for hard things: death and dying, illness and the body
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Euphemisms for death have many jobs: they comfort a family, honour soldiers, sell funerals and make dark jokes.
explain: Businesses rename sad things to sound gentler: an undertaker becomes a "funeral director" and a cemetery becomes a "memorial park".
explain: For soldiers, words like "the fallen" and "made the ultimate sacrifice" show respect for those who died in war.
explain: Euphemisms also soften illness: people say they are "under the weather" instead of "ill", and older people said "the big C" because they were afraid even to say "cancer".
explain: Vivid example: a Londoner who says "he's brown bread" is using rhyming slang, where "bread" rhymes with "dead".
fact: "Met an untimely end" means died young or died violently.
fact: "Took his own life" means killed himself; it is the gentle way to speak of suicide.
fact: "The big sleep" means death; it is a humorous, tough-sounding phrase from crime stories.
fact: "The dearly departed" means the dead; it is polite and is sometimes used jokingly.
fact: "Bit the big one" means died; it is American slang.
fact: "On his last legs" means close to dying or worn out; a car or a sofa can also be on its last legs.
fact: "Memorial park" is an American word for a cemetery.
fact: "Funeral director" is the official name for an undertaker, the person who arranges funerals.
fact: "Pre-need arrangements" is American funeral-business jargon for paying for your own funeral in advance.
fact: "Celebration of life" means a funeral or memorial service with a cheerful mood.
fact: "The fallen" means soldiers killed in war.
fact: "Made the ultimate sacrifice" means died in war while serving one's country.
fact: "Snuffed it" means died; it is British slang, from snuffing out a candle.
fact: "Turned up his toes" means died; it is old-fashioned.
fact: "Brown bread" means dead; it is London rhyming slang ("bread" rhymes with "dead").
fact: "Assumed room temperature" means died; it is a dark American joke, because a dead body cools down.
fact: "Flatlined" means died or the heart stopped, because the heart monitor shows a flat line; a business can also flatline.
fact: "Under the weather" means slightly ill.
fact: "Off colour" means unwell, often looking pale; it is mostly British.
fact: "The big C" means cancer.
exercise: "The young sailor met an untimely end at sea." What does that mean? | He died young, at sea | He arrived late at sea | He finished his trip early | He met a stranger at sea
exercise: A news report says "the singer took his own life". What does it mean? | He killed himself | He moved far away | He started a new life | He took charge of his career
exercise: In an old detective story, "he's gone to the big sleep". What happened to him? | He died | He went to bed early | He fell asleep at work | He fainted
exercise: A comedian says "a toast to the dearly departed!" Who is meant? | The dead | People who just left the party | People who flew away on holiday | People who are dear friends
exercise: An American says "my old truck almost bit the big one". What nearly happened? | It nearly broke down for good | It nearly hit a big truck | It nearly won a race | It nearly got a big dent
exercise: "Grandad's dog is on his last legs." What does that mean? | The dog is old and close to dying | The dog has hurt his back legs | The dog is learning to walk | The dog is running his last race
exercise: An American sign says "Green Hills Memorial Park". What is it? | A cemetery | A children's playground | A museum garden | A sports ground
exercise: What is a "funeral director"? | An undertaker who arranges funerals | A film director who makes sad films | A priest who leads a church | A judge who reads wills
exercise: An American advert says "Make your pre-need arrangements today." What is it selling? | Paying for your own funeral in advance | Insurance for your car | Saving money for a holiday | Buying food before a storm
exercise: "Instead of a funeral, there will be a celebration of life." What is it? | A funeral or memorial with a happier mood | A big birthday party for the oldest person there | A wedding held in the church after the funeral | A school prize day for the best pupils
exercise: At a war memorial: "We will remember the fallen." Who are "the fallen"? | Soldiers killed in war | Soldiers who tripped over | Soldiers who lost a battle | Soldiers who were wounded
exercise: "Private Jones made the ultimate sacrifice." What happened to him? | He died in war | He gave all his money away | He left the army | He gave up his holiday
exercise: A British man says "the old cat finally snuffed it". What happened to the cat? | It died | It sneezed | It sniffed something | It hid
exercise: "The old farmer turned up his toes in the spring." What does this mean? | He died | He went swimming | He took off his boots | He hid
exercise: A Londoner says "the old landlord's brown bread". What does he mean? | The old landlord is dead | The old landlord bakes bread | The old landlord has a tan | The old landlord is broke
exercise: A dark American joke: "the villain assumed room temperature." What happened to the villain? | He died | He went indoors | He calmed down | He sat in the shade
exercise: In a hospital drama, "the patient flatlined." What happened? | The patient's heart stopped | The patient lay down flat | The patient's blood pressure was normal | The patient fell asleep
exercise: "I'm a bit under the weather today." How does the speaker feel? | Slightly ill | Wet from the rain | Too hot | Very happy
exercise: A British mum says "you look a bit off colour, love." What does she mean? | You look unwell | Your clothes don't match | You look sunburnt | Your hair is dyed
exercise: "Auntie had the big C, but she beat it." What illness did she have? | Cancer | A cold | Chickenpox | A cough
test: News: "The young racing driver met an untimely end at Monza." What happened? | The young driver died at Monza | The driver finished last at Monza | The driver came late to Monza | The driver retired at Monza
test: Chatty: "School's out, I'm under the weather anyway, staying in." Why is the speaker staying in? | They feel a bit ill | It is raining | They are scared of storms | They are being punished
test: Ellie says "the fallen means soldiers who fell off their horses, right?" What is true? | No, it means soldiers who were killed in war | Yes, it means riders who fell | No, it means soldiers who lost a battle | Yes, it means soldiers who tripped
test: Dad: "This sofa's on its last legs, we'll need a new one." What is true about the sofa? | It is old and nearly worn out | It has only one leg left | It is going to be moved | It was bought last
test: A Londoner in a film: "Cross me and you're brown bread." What kind of remark is it? | A threat to kill, in rhyming slang | An offer of some fresh food from his kitchen | A joke about a sunburn | A compliment about baking
test: A notice: "Mr Lee's family invite friends to a celebration of life on Saturday." What is happening? | A memorial for Mr Lee, who has died | Mr Lee's birthday party | Mr Lee's wedding | Mr Lee's retirement party
test: Gossip: "Uncle Jim? Snuffed it last year, God rest him." What do you learn? | Uncle Jim died last year | Uncle Jim caught a cold last year | Uncle Jim stopped smoking last year | Uncle Jim moved last year
test: News: "Sales of the new phone have flatlined." What does "flatlined" mean here? | Sales stopped growing | Sales went up fast | The phone died | The phone went flat
test: A grave says "In memory of our dearly departed mother." Who is buried there? | The family's dead mother | A mother who moved abroad | A mother who left the family | A dear neighbour
test: A teacher says "Josh is a bit off colour, so he went home." Why did Josh go home? | He felt unwell | He spilt paint on his clothes | He was naughty | His uniform was the wrong colour
test: Grandma says "When I was young, we didn't say cancer, we said the big C." Why did people do that? | They were afraid to say the frightening word | It was shorter to write | It was a medical code | It was a kind of letter game
test: Which phrase about death would you use in a polite speech, not a joke? | Made the ultimate sacrifice | Assumed room temperature | Pushing up the daisies | Gone to the big sleep
exam: Identify the plain meaning of the designation "funeral director". | An undertaker who arranges funerals | A person who directs films concerning death | A cleric who conducts religious services | An official who registers deaths
exam: It is given that a commemorative inscription refers to "the fallen". Determine to whom the inscription refers. | Soldiers killed in war | Soldiers wounded in war | Soldiers who deserted their posts | Soldiers who returned home
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises the expression "under the weather". | An informal expression signifying mild illness | A meteorological term signifying heavy rain | A formal term signifying a grave illness | An expression signifying shelter from a storm
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the expression "pre-need arrangements" as employed in commercial funeral literature. | Payment for one's own funeral in advance | Preparations made before a storm | Provisions for the needs of children | Arrangements for a medical procedure
